(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609093400-cleanup-three-ledger-events-and-one-revert-risk")
 (:adr/title "Cleanup 2026-09-09: three ledger events rescued, one revert-risk PR stopped")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-09")
 (:adr/authority "owner instruction, 2026-09-09 (git cleanup conflict, then closing)")
 (:adr/repos "com-junkawasaki/root, net-kotobase/control-plane, cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5610, kotoba-lang/org-wi-fi-easy-connect, network-awai/app-hyakka")
 (:adr/body "Cleanup session, 2026-09-09, invoked by the owner as `git cleanup conflict`
then closed with `closing`. Skill: git-cleanup-conflict. Everything below was
measured; nothing was inferred from a tool's summary line alone.

## What was rescued

Three append-only ledger events existed in no commit and on no remote.

All six superproject stashes were `90-docs/business/canvas-ledger.edn`
appends. Judged on the semantic key required by `:append-only-ledger-gate`
(the line with `:event/at` stripped, because `kagami reconcile` re-stamps that
field on every run and a line-exact test over-reports), nine of twelve distinct
events were already on `origin/main`. Three were not: `:seq` 14416, 14417,
14418, all stamped 2026-09-08T20:34. Seq 14415 and 14419 are both present on
main, so this was a real three-event hole rather than a numbering artifact.

The gate forbids appending a missing event at the tail without checking the
fold, because `gftd.canvas/fold` replays in file order and an old observation
appended after a newer one can overwrite newer canvas state. That hazard does
not apply to these three. `70-tools/bmc/src/gftd/canvas.cljc` `apply-event` is
a `case` over `:event/type` with clauses for `:canvas/add-item`,
`:canvas/retract-item`, `:canvas/note` and `:hyp/status` only; its default arm
returns `idx` unchanged, and its own docstring says observation events are
no-ops. The three events are two `:react/observation` and one
`:governor/rejected`. All three are fold no-ops, so tail order cannot matter.

Landed one line at a time through `scripts/ledger-land.cljs` (one line at a
time because that script tests idempotency with `str/includes?`, so a
multi-line payload double-appends when its first line is already present).
Verified after landing: main went 10852 to 10855 lines, each of the three
seqs appears exactly once, and all 10855 lines parse as EDN.

## What was stopped

`net-kotobase/control-plane#653` would have made the default branch factually
wrong. `cleanup-land` found an untracked `kotobase-head-store/README.md`,
correctly declined to auto-merge it (it exists on main with different content)
and opened a draft PR. Three measurements closed it:

- the checkout it came from, `orgs/network-awai/net-kotobase`, is rename
  residue: its remote `network-awai/net-kotobase` redirects to
  `net-kotobase/control-plane`, which west already carries at
  `orgs/net-kotobase/control-plane`;
- that checkout's HEAD is `f2d38219`, dated 2026-08-10, roughly a month stale;
- the diff replaces a true statement with a false one. It rewrites `The schema
  is contracts/architecture/head-store-shards.schema.json.` into `This
  historical format is not a contract under contracts/architecture.` That file
  exists on main today, 1251 bytes, checked through the contents API.

Deletions were 3, not 0, so it was never in the auto-merge class to begin with:
`:preservation-pr-disposition` puts any preservation PR with a deleted line in
`:needs-review`, and a stale base that reverses a live claim in
`:close-stale-revert-risk`. Closed with the reasoning in a comment, content
archived verbatim, and the path added to that checkout's `.git/info/exclude`
so the next `--apply` does not recreate the same PR. Verified both ways: the
file is no longer a landing candidate, and the archive holds its bytes.

Two things were deliberately NOT done, both cases where a tool's own output
pointed the wrong way:

- `cleanup-land` warned that `orgs/network-awai/net-kotobase` landed while
  west-unregistered and printed a `west-triple-sync` command. That check reads
  only whether the path appears in `west.yml`; it does not ask whether the repo
  is registered under its successor name. It is. Re-registering the dead path
  is exactly what the `:renamed-upstream` row of the west-orphan table forbids.
- Two worktrees reported mass deletions, one with 229,802 ` D ` entries. Both
  are cone-mode sparse checkouts and the sampled paths exist on `origin/main`.
  Applying those deletions would have wiped the tree.

## Landed and retired

- `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5610#3` merged, one 103-byte doc sample,
  verified present on that repo's main after merge.
- Stashes 6 to 0. All archived before any drop.
- Local branches 37 to 21. Fourteen merged branches deleted with `git branch
  -d`, which refuses unmerged branches; all fourteen succeeded, which is itself
  the confirmation that the merged verdict was right.
- `west-orphan-audit --blocking` went exit 1 to exit 0 with
  `renamed-UNVERIFIED: 0`, so the zero is an answer rather than an unanswerable
  run. The blocking edge was `kotoba-net`: registered correctly in west, the
  checkout simply absent, fixed with a targeted `west update --fetch smart
  kotoba-net`.
- `kotoba-lang/org-wi-fi-easy-connect` registered in west; it was pushed to
  GitHub and never registered, the classic GH-only orphan.
- `scripts/worktree-retire.cljs --apply`: 5 retired, 35 stale entries pruned,
  0 failed.

## Gaps left open, in priority order

1. **3,590 un-landed fleet-db ledger events.** In the worktree
   `/private/tmp/itonami-spam-release`, branch `codex/spam-release-20260909`,
   `manifest/fleet-db.ledger.edn` carries 3,590 uncommitted events (3,287
   `:pin/reconcile-legacy`, 303 `:repo/added-legacy`) that are the output of an
   uncommitted `kagami reconcile`. All 3,590 are missing from `origin/main` on
   the semantic key. NOT landed by cleanup: CLAUDE.md forbids hand-editing that
   file (append-only, signed VCS plane), `:append-only-ledger-gate` assigns
   `:pin/reconcile-legacy` to fleet reconcile, and appending risks writing pin
   regressions if the reconcile input was not `origin/main`'s `west.yml` (the
   2026-08-05 incident). Archived with provenance. Resume: re-run reconcile with
   `git show origin/main:manifest/west.yml` as the input and let it land through
   its own path, or judge the run stale and discard.

2. **A bot committed conflict markers to a pushed branch.** In
   `orgs/network-awai/app-hyakka`, the tip of `bot/gp-source-20260904g`
   contains `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>` inside
   `src/hyakka/corpus/world_legal.cljc` at lines 622, 739 and 892. Nothing is
   at risk right now: that branch is an ancestor of `origin/main` (verified on
   full history, the repo is not shallow) and main's copy is clean at 889
   lines. The defect is that the bot which produced it can produce it again.
   The working-tree edit in that checkout removes 143 lines main still has, so
   it was archived and left untouched rather than landed.

3. **`codex/maturity-remeasure` is local-only and unpushed.** The only branch
   in this superproject existing on no remote. Judged superseded rather than
   un-landed: main implements the same optimisation in generalised form, and
   main's comment reads `ranked 行にだけ後付けする` where the branch reads
   `ranked 5 本にだけ後付けする`, so main derived from it. NOT deleted, because
   discarding the only copy of anything on a judgement call is the owner's to
   make. Held in worktree `root-maturity-remeasure-mtql61p3`.

4. **88 UNLANDED child repos remain.** `nbb scripts/cleanup.cljs --unlanded`
   reported 88 of 4416 scanned, and the run completed (its own completion line
   was present, so it was not killed mid-scan). This session handled only the
   seven holding untracked content, which is the top of the ladder because
   untracked work is on no branch and no remote. The rest are `nopr` and
   `unpushed` rows and several branch farms.

5. **The daily worktree-retire job is still not installed.** CLAUDE.md already
   records that `cloud.itonami.bot.worktree-retire` is named in the roster but
   never loaded; that is still true, which is why 35 stale entries and 5
   eligible worktrees had accumulated for this session to clear by hand.

6. **57 `nexus-x402-supporter-*` worktrees** under
   `~/.cloud-itonami/awai-worktrees/awai/`, one roughly every 100 minutes, all
   landed and clean and all younger than the 7-day floor, so `worktree-retire`
   correctly keeps every one. The floor is not the problem; the creation rate
   is.

## Validation actually run

- `nbb scripts/west-orphan-audit.cljs --blocking`: exit 0,
  `local-root-broken 0`, `relative-paths-broken 0`, `true-orphan-git 0`,
  `renamed-UNVERIFIED 0`.
- EDN readability of the landed ledger: 10855 lines, 0 parse failures.
- Conflict-marker search over tracked `manifest/`, `90-docs/`, `scripts/`:
  none.
- `worktree-retire --apply`: SCANNED 1516, retired 5, kept 1457, unverified 19,
  pruned 35, failed 0.
- Superproject: on `main`, 0 ahead 0 behind `origin/main`, 0 stashes, 0 open
  PRs.

Not run, and named rather than skipped silently: `gen-west-manifest.cljs
--check` was not used as a gate, because it compares against a wholesale
regeneration from local child-repo HEADs and reports STALE as its normal
reading in any shared checkout; the manifest changes in this session went
through the single-entry path, which is the shape that check cannot judge.

## Archive

Everything dropped or closed in this session is recoverable from
`.git/stash-archive-20260909/` in the superproject: 14 stash patches with an
`index.txt`, 14 branch archives under `branches/`, and the un-landed ledger
events under `unlanded-ledger-events-20260909/` with a provenance file naming
the worktree and branch they came from. Per-repo archives were written by
`cleanup-land` into each child repo's own `.git/stash-archive-20260909/`.

One process note worth carrying forward. While writing that archive this
session truncated a pre-existing `index.txt` from an earlier session the same
day; the three lost entries were reconstructed from still-reachable objects and
all 14 patches were intact, but the correct move is to append to an existing
archive index rather than recreate it.
")
))
